 ## Overview
-Vector Quantization (VQ) is a lossy data compression algorithm that maps a set of $n$-dimensional input vectors to a single fixed-length index referencing an entry in an $n$-dimensional codebook [S1, S3]. It solves the problem of storing large volumes of high-dimensional data, such as voxel color features or time-varying volumetric blocks, by exploiting redundancy [S2]. Instead of storing every vector individually, it stores a compact codebook and a mapping index for each data element [S1, S2]. The method is characterized as a fixed-to-fixed compression algorithm [S1].
+Vector Quantization (VQ) is a lossy data compression algorithm that maps a set of $n$-dimensional input vectors to a single fixed-length index referencing an entry in an $n$-dimensional codebook [S1, S3]. It solves the problem of storing large volumes of high-dimensional data, such as voxel color features or time-varying volumetric blocks, by exploiting spatial and temporal redundancy [S2]. Instead of storing every vector individually, it stores a compact codebook and a mapping index for each data element [S1, S2]. The method is characterized as a fixed-to-fixed compression algorithm [S1].
 
